9. Cardinality

Cardinality describes how many records in one table/entity can be associated with records in another.

9.1 One-to-One (1:1)

One record is associated with one record.

Example:

Person → Passport

Assuming one person has one passport and one passport belongs to one person.

9.2 One-to-Many (1:M)

One record can be associated with many records.

Example:

Customer → Orders

One customer can have many orders.

Asha
 ├── Order 101
 └── Order 102
9.3 Many-to-One (M:1)

Many records are associated with one record.

Example:

Orders → Customer

Many orders can belong to one customer.

1:M and M:1 can describe the same relationship depending on the direction.

9.4 Many-to-Many (M:M)

Many records can be associated with many records.

Example:

Students ↔ Courses

One student can take many courses, and one course can have many students.

A relational database typically represents this using a bridge/junction table:

STUDENTS
    ↓
STUDENT_COURSE
    ↓
COURSES
Memory
1:1 → One has one
1:M → One has many
M:1 → Many belong to one
M:M → Many have many
